Douglas Berry wrote:
> I recently have started playing a lot of monks for some reason, and
> since these tend to be quick deaths for me, I was wondering if anyone
> had advice about playing them?

I've been playing monks recently,  and I thought I'd add a tip:

Did you know your monk is always guaranteed to start with Charm Horse?


He always starts with a ton of apples, oranges et al.  These don't keep
a player alive very long, so I've learned to subsist on food rations
and save the fruit for warhorses et al.  Those suckers can hit hard.
So I use the monk's starting fruit to tame them and now I have a
powerful ally in the dungeon instead of an enemy.  They tend to starve
to death, of course, but they are replaceable.

I've learned to treat tripe rations the same way -- as a disguised
scroll of Tame Dog or Tame Cat.    Between the fruit and the tripe
rations,  the early levels are abrim with potential allies in the form
of dogs, cats and horses.   Tame a bunch of meat shields to protect the
early monk (who stinks at melee and should stay out of it) and get an
easy way of identifying cursed stuff in the bargain.

